      <section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Understand cost per lead</h2><p>Cost per lead is advertising spend divided by attributed leads. It measures inquiry volume relative to spending, but says nothing by itself about service-area fit or customer quality.</p><p>A click, a form submission, an answered call and a confirmed booking are different events. Reporting should not treat them as interchangeable.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Calculate the cost per qualified inquiry</h2><p>Define qualification using relevant service, valid contact information, service territory and a clear customer need. This provides a more useful comparison than raw inquiry volume.</p><p>For example, a hypothetical $1,800 campaign generating 36 inquiries has a $50 cost per inquiry. If only 18 qualify, the qualified cost is $100. These numbers are examples, not market benchmarks.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Measure confirmed appointments</h2><p>Track actual scheduled service visits and estimate appointments separately. Where possible, verify bookings through the CRM or booking provider.</p><p>If the hypothetical $1,800 campaign produces nine confirmed appointments, cost per booking is $200. That still does not establish profitability without tracking completed work.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Account for different job types</h2><p>A drain repair, water heater replacement and commercial plumbing project can have very different labor, materials, overhead and expected contribution margins.</p><p>Compare acquisition cost against the economics of each service type. A higher-cost installation inquiry may be valuable even when a cheaper repair lead produces less revenue.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Include the full cost of acquisition</h2><p>Advertising costs are only one component. Landing-page development, creative, software, agency fees and sales-response labor may also affect total acquisition cost.</p><p>Separate one-time setup expenses from ongoing expenses and evaluate actual contribution margin rather than assuming job revenue is profit.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Use real data to decide when to scale</h2><p>Review qualified leads, bookings, attended visits, completed work and net contribution after delivery costs. Use those results to establish realistic acquisition targets.</p><p>Costs vary by market, competition and service type. There is no universal plumbing CPL figure that guarantees a profitable campaign.</p></section>

      <section class="lf-article-section">
